import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { doc, repo } from "../lib/links";

const CHECKED = { date: "4 October 2026", revision: "02fb6a2" };
---

<Page
  title="Project status"
  description="luciole is experimental and published on npm: tested on macOS and Linux. What is tested, what is not, the pinned versions and how breaking changes are announced."
>
  <h1>Project status</h1>
  <p class="meta">
    Checked on {CHECKED.date} against the repository at revision <a
      href={`${repo}/tree/${CHECKED.revision}`}><code>{CHECKED.revision}</code></a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you try it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Experimental, version 0.x.</strong> An experiment run in the open. Until 1.0, a minor
      release may break the API.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Published on npm.</strong> Start with <code>bunx luciole.sh init my-app</code>. Five
      packages share one version: <code>luciole.sh</code>, <code>@luciole-sh/core</code>,
      <code>@luciole-sh/create</code>, <code>@luciole-sh/flow-graph</code> and
      <code>@luciole-sh/markdown-editor</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bun 1.4.2 or newer</strong>, as the <code>engines</code> field says. CI runs 1.4.2,
      pinned in <code>.bun-version</code>. It runs on <strong>macOS and Linux</strong>; no other
      system is tested, Windows included.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Desktop app: a prototype.</strong> macOS arm64 only, unsigned, not published.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Changes and versions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Patch releases (0.1.x) do not break the API.</li>
    <li>
      A minor release (0.x) may break it. Each break is listed in the <a
        href={`${repo}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`}>changelog</a
      >, marked <strong>Breaking</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Only the latest release gets security fixes. See the <a
        href={`${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>security policy</a
      >.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is tested</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      On every push and pull request, GitHub Actions on macOS and Linux: types, lint, format,
      integration tests and build, a production journey in a real PTY, the dev server's rebuild
      journey, and a clean install that creates a starter and drives its client in a PTY.
    </li>
    <li>
      On Linux in CI, each of the five packages is packed, installed in an empty project and
      imported, and its licences are checked. The release workflow runs the same checks before it
      publishes.
    </li>
    <li>
      On Linux in CI, the compiled client in Debian (glibc) and Alpine (musl) containers without
      Bun, and the sandbox mode in containers, one per mechanism (namespaces, bubblewrap, Landlock).
    </li>
    <li>
      More PTY journeys, one per example and launch path, and browser journeys for the web builds,
      run from the repository: <code>bun run test:pty:&lt;name&gt;</code>,
      <code>bun run test:web</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The remote path was tested by hand, a macOS client and a Linux server over an SSH tunnel.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      No WAN measurement campaign, no keystroke-to-screen latency measured on a physical display,
      no multi-user load, no public TLS proxy. The latency in the site's demo is simulated, per
      request.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One user per server</strong> by default: the built-in session is a single token.
      Multi-user sign-in is the app's to write, in <code>server/auth.ts</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No Fast Refresh.</strong> A rebuild restarts both processes: the route, history and
      named fields come back, state held only in memory does not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No silent retries.</strong> The framework never retries an operation whose outcome is
      unknown; the app decides what to do (Notes looks it up, then resends it with the same id). <code>useLive</code> streams do not reconnect by themselves, nor
      does a client joined by an <code>ssh://</code> URL; a server started with <code>--on</code>
      has its tunnel relaunched.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pinned versions</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>React, React Flight</dt>
    <dd>19.3.0, exactly</dd>
    <dt>OpenTUI</dt>
    <dd>0.5.12, exactly, with react-reconciler 0.33.0 (OpenTUI's range)</dd>
    <dt>TanStack Router</dt>
    <dd>1.170.38</dd>
    <dt>Bun</dt>
    <dd>1.4.2 in CI; 1.4.2 or newer to use luciole</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    The React Flight adapter is isolated in <code>packages/core/src/flight/</code>; any upgrade of
    React means retesting it.
  </p>

  <h2>Distribution and signing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The five packages are published with npm provenance. The examples are not packages: they
      run from git, at the tag of the release (<code>luciole example &lt;name&gt;</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      A compiled macOS binary carries an ad hoc signature: it runs where it was built or copied
      with <code>scp</code>, and Gatekeeper blocks one downloaded by a browser.
    </li>
    <li>
      Signing and notarization for macOS are implemented (<code>--sign</code>,
      <code>--notarize</code>) but notarization has never run end to end: no Developer ID was
      available. Publishing on npm does not change this.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="meta">
    Evidence. The design notes are in French: <a href={doc("VALIDATION.md")}>VALIDATION.md</a> (what
    is tested, and the known limits), <a href={doc("DISTRIBUTION.md")}>DISTRIBUTION.md</a> (builds
    and signing). In English: <a href={`${repo}#what-runs-where`}>what runs where, in the README</a>
    and the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/.github/workflows/ci.yml`}>CI workflow</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
